 "title": "Fall of Constantinople",
 "date_label": "6 April - 29 May 1453",
 "year": 1453,
 "intro": "In the spring of 1453 the twenty-one-year-old Sultan Mehmed II besieged Constantinople, the last real stronghold of the Byzantine Empire and a city that had stood behind its walls for a thousand years. The defenders, about eight thousand strong including Genoese, Venetian, Catalan and other volunteers under Emperor Constantine XI, faced an army that Western accounts number at around a hundred thousand, together with a large fleet and an unusual train of heavy guns. The land walls on the west held for seven weeks against bombardment, assaults and mining, and the Golden Horn was sealed by a chain until the Ottomans dragged ships over the hill of Galata. Before dawn on 29 May the last attack broke through, helped by the wounding of the Genoese captain Giustiniani, and the city fell.",
 "outcome": "Ottoman victory: Constantinople taken, Constantine XI killed, the Byzantine Empire ended.",
 "uncertainty": "Troop figures are contemporary estimates and vary widely, as do fleet sizes and gun counts. The land wall line is drawn through the gate positions from map data and is approximate in between. The chain's line, the overland track of the ships, the reserve positions and the miners' sectors are reconstructions. Constantine's death and the performance of the Orban gun are disputed in the sources.",
